hi this is presh talwalkar  today's puzzle is something you might  see on a technical interview question  imagine you have three people and 11  identical coins  how many ways are there to split 11  coins to three people  only the total number of coins to each  person matters so you might give five  coins to blue six coins to red and no  coins to green  part two of the question  how many ways are there if each person  has to get at least one coin  now five six zero would not be a  solution because we have to give green  at least one coin so might give green  one red five and blue five  give these problems a try and when  you're ready keep watching the video for  the solutions  we'll start out by solving the number of  ways you can split 11 coins to three  people  we'll use a trick which is to place two  dividers between the coins  we'll color one divider blue and another  divider red  the number of coins to the left of the  blue divider will be the number of coins  that blue gets the number of coins  between the dividers will be the number  of coins that red gets and the number of  coins to the right of the red divider  will be the number of coin that greens  gets  so if i place the dividers in the  following positions  there are three coins to the left of the  blue divider that go to blue  there are six coins in between the  divider that's how many red gets and  they're two coins to the right of the  red divider which go to green  we can change the positions of these  dividers  if i move the blue divider over  now blue's getting five coins red is  getting four coins and green is getting  two coins  i can change the positions again if i  move the blue divider all the way over  to the left now there are no coins to  the left of the blue divider so blue is  getting zero coins in between the  dividers is seven coins that go to red  and to the right of the red divider is  four coins that go to green  imagine i shift the dividers over like  this  now blue is getting six coins  the two dividers are next to each other  there are no coins so that means red  gets no coins and green gets the  remaining five coins  i'll let you figure out how to have a  division where green gets zero coins  but basically we can count the number of  ways to split 11 coins to three  identical people by figuring out the  number of ways we can place these two  dividers  so imagine we have 11 plus two which is  13 total slots  we're going to place the dividers in two  of these slots and the coins in the  remaining slots  so we want to pick two of the spots for  the dividers  and then we place the remaining  slots we fill them with the coins  so we can count the number of ways to  split 11 coins to three people by  counting the number of ways that we can  pick the two dividers in 13 slots  that's the number of ways you can choose  two elements from 13 which is a binomial  coefficient 13 choose 2 which ends up  being 78 so that's part one answer is  that you can have 78 ways to split 11  coins three people  let's generalize this  let's assume we have n identical items  that we want to divide to our people  as we did before we basically want to  use r minus 1 dividers  we're going to need to draw out n plus r  minus 1 slots of which we're going to  pick r minus 1 dividers  so it's the number of ways that we can  choose r minus 1 elements from  n plus r minus 1  which will be n plus r minus 1 choose r  minus 1 which is equivalently n plus r  minus 1 choose n  there's another way you can think about  the solution  imagine the algebraic equation x1 plus  x2 all the way up to xr  are variables being equal to n and we  want to find the number of non-negative  integer solutions  this is exactly partitioning n into r  different spaces  so this also gives you the amount of  distinct non-negative integer solutions  to this equation you can interpret each  term x i is the amount that person i  gets  so now let's proceed to the next  question which is to do the same split  except each person has to get at least  one coin  now we don't actually have to start over  what we're going to do is a trick where  we start out by giving one coin to each  person so now we know each person has at  least one coins  now we want to divide up the remaining  eight coins  to the three people and we can do that  by dividing the eight coins using our  two divider method  so we want to divide  in the same exact way but we're just  going to add  i'll just do one example to show you how  this works imagine i put the dividers  here there's one coin to the left of the  blue divider but blue already has one  coin so blue ends up with two coins  now in between the divider  there are six coins but then we're going  to add the one that red already has  to the right of the red divider dividers  one coin we add on the one that green  already has to get two  so we can find all ways to split 11  coins three people where each person  gets at least one  by giving each person one coin and then  using our two divider method on the  eight coins remaining  so let's go through this solution  we start out by giving one coin to each  so that means they're 11 minus three  which is eight coins  we then  have to solve the problem of dividing  those eight coins into three people  so we're going to have eight plus two  which is ten slots of which we're going  to pick the two spots for the divisors  the dividers  as we just solved this will be the  number of ways to choose two elements  from 10 which is 10 choose 2  which becomes 45.  we can generalize this  let's imagine we want to split n  identical coins to our people so that  each person gets at least one coin  we start out by giving one coin to each  of the r people so they're n minus our  coins remaining  then we want to divide the n minus our  coins to our people  we've already solved this before you use  r minus one divisors for a total of n  minus 1 spots  so this becomes the number of ways to  choose r minus 1 elements from n minus 1  which is n minus 1 choose r minus 1.  algebraically if you have the solution  x1 plus x2 all the way up to xr equaling  xn equaling n  we now can solve for the number of  positive integer solutions using n minus  1 choose r minus one and in this case we  again interpret x i as the amount that  person i gets  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description

